It uses hazeljs (https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazeljs, MIT), loaded from npm, and frames Hazel's own editor
(https://hazel.org, MIT) from a branch build at build.hazel.org.

The hexagrams' order (King Wen) and names are traditional. The names here are short glosses of our own and common
renderings of the Chinese titles.

The Tarot's woodcut faces (tarot/woodcut, a git submodule, not a copy) are Woodcut tarot, https://github.com/SONDLecT/woodcut-tarot,
by way of our fork, https://github.com/matthewhammer/woodcut-tarot, at commit e017dd5c8e: dedicated to the public domain
under CC0 1.0, and so not under this repository's licences. Its 78 cards are traced from the Jean Noblet tarot (Paris,
c. 1650; Bibliotheque nationale de France, RESERVE KH-34), with the Six to Ten of Wands from the Jacques Vieville tarot
(Paris, c. 1650), from the BnF's scans on Gallica; the colour set's small red ovals are the BnF's ownership stamps.

The pages link to other sites for fuller meanings (Cafe au Soul for the I Ching; Biddy Tarot, by Brigit Esselmont,
and Labyrinthos, by Tina Gong, for the Tarot's cards). Those pages and their text are their publishers'; nothing of
theirs is copied here.
